WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
Fleet management begins with an authoritative inventory of aircraft, controllers,

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.040
batteries, payloads, chargers, accessories, firmware, ownership, location, and status.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.635
Each asset needs a unique identifier and responsible custodian.

00:00:12.635 --> 00:00:16.510
Maintenance logs capture inspections, flight hours or cycles where relevant, defects,

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.849
repairs, parts, updates, incidents, and return-to-service approval.

00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:23.780
Records should show the configuration that actually flew, not only the model purchased.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.200
Batteries need individual tracking because age, cycles, storage, temperature, damage,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.559
swelling, and voltage behavior affect safety.

00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:34.580
A battery should not inherit an aircraft's general ready status without its own condition

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:35.140
review.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.099
Preflight inspections confirm readiness for the specific mission.

00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:42.480
Post-flight inspections look for damage, contamination, heat, unusual sound, impact,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.800
or performance changes.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.080
Findings should enter a repair or grounding workflow immediately.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.582
Firmware and application updates require governance.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.890
Record versions, review significant changes, test as appropriate,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.770
and avoid unplanned configuration drift across crews.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.470
Updating everything immediately before a critical mission can create its own risk.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.595
Ground unsafe or uncertain equipment.

00:01:01.595 --> 00:01:05.248
Mission urgency, sunk cost, and spare shortages do not change airworthiness.

00:01:05.248 --> 00:01:09.240
Lifecycle management should anticipate obsolescence, unsupported software,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.940
repair lead time, replacement, and secure retirement.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.110
Maintain an approved configuration baseline for aircraft, controller, application,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.880
payload, and critical settings.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.180
A replacement component or reset may look ready while silently changing return behavior,

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.180
units, storage, or warning thresholds.
